cedDate":"2025-04-30","latestActionDate":"2026-07-15","latestActionText":"Ordered to be Reported in the Nature of a Substitute by the Yeas and Nays: 39 - 0.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Congressional oversight","Health care costs and insurance","Health care coverage and access","Health care quality","Health technology, devices, supplies","Medicare"],"sponsorBioguideId":"K000392","cosponsorCount":9,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/3108","industries":[{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Hospitals and physician groups that offer remote monitoring services must meet new real-time availability and data reporting requirements to receive Medicare payment."},{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Medical device manufacturers that produce remote patient monitoring equipment face new technical and operational requirements for Medicare reimbursement."},{"industry":"tech","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Software and IT service providers that develop remote monitoring platforms and electronic health record integration systems are affected by the compatibility and data transmission mandates."},{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Medicare Advantage plans and other insurers may see changes in reimbursement patterns and cost structures for remote monitoring services."}],"plainSummary":"This bill sets new conditions for Medicare to pay for remote patient monitoring services. It requires health care practitioners to be available in real time to respond to problems detected by monitoring devices, mandates that monitoring systems transmit data in formats compatible with electronic health records, and requires reporting to help evaluate cost savings. The bill also establishes a minimum payment level for these services and directs the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services to report on costs and savings over four years.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoI